<BaseLayout title="Acupressure for High Blood Pressure: Points for Relaxation Support" description="Acupressure points traditionally used to support relaxation with high blood pressure: GV20, LV3, PC6, HT7, and KD3, with important safety guidance." breadcrumbs={[{ "label": "Home", "href": "/" }, { "label": "Conditions", "href": "/conditions/" }, { "label": "Acupressure for High Blood Pressure", "href": "/conditions/acupressure-for-high-blood-pressure/" }]} datePublished="2026-08-27" dateModified="2026-08-27" articleSchema={true}>
  <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-12 lg:px-6"><h1 class="mb-4">Acupressure for High Blood Pressure: Points for Relaxation Support</h1><p class="text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 text-lg mb-8">High blood pressure (hypertension) is a serious medical condition that needs proper diagnosis and treatment. Some points are traditionally used to help the body relax, which can be a small supportive habit alongside your prescribed care. This guide is clear about its limits: acupressure cannot lower blood pressure reliably and must never replace medication.</p><div class="mb-8 p-4 bg-red-50 dark:bg-red-900/20 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-lg"><p class="text-sm text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300"><strong>Important:</strong> If you have high blood pressure, follow your clinician's treatment plan and keep taking any prescribed medication. Acupressure is at most a relaxation support. Seek emergency care for a reading that is very high (commonly cited as 180/120 or above) or for symptoms such as chest pain, breathlessness, vision changes, or a severe headache.</p></div><section id="gv20" class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4"><a href="/points/gv20-baihui-acupressure-point/" class="text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300">1. GV20 (Baihui) - Hundred Meetings</a></h2><p class="mb-3">GV20 at the crown is traditionally used to calm and clear the head, which can support a sense of relaxation.</p><p class="mb-3"><strong>Location:</strong> On the midline of the head, about two finger-widths behind the hairline at the top of the skull. <a href="/points/gv20-baihui-acupressure-point/">GV20 guide</a>.</p><p class="mb-3"><strong>Technique:</strong> Gentle steady pressure with the fingertips for 1-2 minutes.</p></section><section id="lv3" class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4"><a href="/points/lv3-taichong-acupressure-point/" class="text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300">2. LV3 (Taichong) - Great Surge</a></h2><p class="mb-3">LV3 on the foot is traditionally used to ease tension and irritability, common companions of a stressed nervous system.</p><p class="mb-3"><strong>Location:</strong> On the top of the foot, in the hollow between the first and second toes. <a href="/points/lv3-taichong-acupressure-point/">LV3 guide</a>.</p><p class="mb-3"><strong>Technique:</strong> Press firmly into the hollow for 1-2 minutes on each foot.</p></section><section id="pc6" class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4"><a href="/points/pc6-neiguan-acupressure-point/" class="text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300">3. PC6 (Neiguan) - Inner Gate</a></h2><p class="mb-3">PC6 on the inner forearm is traditionally used to calm the chest and mind, supporting slower breathing and a steadier pulse feel.</p><p class="mb-3"><strong>Location:</strong> About three finger-widths above the inner wrist crease, between the two forearm tendons. <a href="/points/pc6-neiguan-acupressure-point/">PC6 guide</a>.</p><p class="mb-3"><strong>Technique:</strong> Press firmly for 1-2 minutes on each arm, breathing slowly.</p></section><section id="ht7" class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4"><a href="/points/ht7-shenmen-acupressure-point/" class="text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300">4. HT7 (Shenmen) - Spirit Gate</a></h2><p class="mb-3">HT7 on the wrist is traditionally used to settle a racing mind and support relaxation, a useful addition to a wind-down routine.</p><p class="mb-3"><strong>Location:</strong> At the wrist crease, on the little-finger side, in the small hollow by the tendon. <a href="/points/ht7-shenmen-acupressure-point/">HT7 guide</a>.</p><p class="mb-3"><strong>Technique:</strong> Press firmly for 1-2 minutes on each wrist.</p></section><section id="kd3" class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4"><a href="/points/kd3-taixi-acupressure-point/" class="text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300">5. KD3 (Taixi) - Great Ravine</a></h2><p class="mb-3">KD3 on the inner ankle is traditionally used to nourish and ground, often included in routines aimed at calming the system.</p><p class="mb-3"><strong>Location:</strong> In the depression between the inner ankle bone and the Achilles tendon. <a href="/points/kd3-taixi-acupressure-point/">KD3 guide</a>.</p><p class="mb-3"><strong>Technique:</strong> Press gently for 1-2 minutes on each ankle.</p></section><section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Frequently Asked Questions</h2><div class="space-y-4"><details class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900"><summary class="font-semibold cursor-pointer text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">Can acupressure replace my blood pressure medication?</summary><p class="mt-2 text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300">No. Hypertension is a medical condition that requires treatment guided by a clinician. Acupressure may help you relax but does not reliably lower blood pressure and should never be used to stop or delay prescribed medication.</p></details><details class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900"><summary class="font-semibold cursor-pointer text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">When is it an emergency?</summary><p class="mt-2 text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300">Seek urgent care for a very high reading (often 180/120 or above) or for chest pain, breathlessness, vision changes, confusion, or a severe headache. These can be signs of a hypertensive crisis.</p></details></div></section><hr class="my-8"><div class="bg-stone-100 dark:bg-stone-800 rounded-lg p-6 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400"><p><strong>Medical Disclaimer:</strong> This content is for informational purposes only and is not medical advice. Never use acupressure instead of prescribed treatment for high blood pressure. Consult your healthcare provider.</p></div>
  <EvidenceBox refs={["lee-frazier-2011", "song-2015"]} note={"Evidence for acupressure from published systematic reviews. See our research page for details."} />
